                <pb facs="0987" n="979" sameAs="frus:frus1938v02/pg979"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">867N.01/1237: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister Resident in Iraq</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Knabenshue</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Baghdad</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-11-02T14:00:00+03:00">November 2, 1938—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 2—10:48 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>44. I explained verbally to the Minister for Foreign Affairs the United States
                    Government’s position in respect to Palestine as suggested <pb facs="0988"
                        n="980" xml:id="pg980"/> in the Department’s telegram No. 21, October 27, 9
                    p.m. [<hi rendition="#italic">7 p.m.</hi>]<anchor n="3" xml:id="d804-f1"/> He
                    replied as follows. He would be glad to secure the support of the United States
                    for the Palestine Arab cause in the present crisis, but if not possible he would
                    of course hope that such support would not be accorded to the Jews, and that the
                    United States Government would not intervene with the British Government and
                    adversely influence the decision which he believes it is about to make. He would
                    be satisfied he said to have the American policy, as recently defined by the
                    Secretary of State, remain the same, no more no less.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Knabenshue</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
